  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Exploring the Transnational Neighbourhood shows early signs of reach through open access use and policy-relevant framing. The book has been downloaded 1,957 times, is available in hybrid open access, and aligns with the SDG theme of Reduced inequalities. Its record is still light on citation evidence, so the strongest signals at present are usage, accessibility, and interdisciplinary relevance.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2022, Exploring the Transnational Neighbourhood brings together geography, anthropology, and literary and cultural studies to rethink how urban neighbourhoods are understood in relation to migration, multiculturalism, and social difference. The book\u2019s subject matter is strongly interdisciplinary, with concept signals spanning sociology, cultural geography, media studies, anthropology, gender studies, and related social-science fields. That breadth is consistent with a work designed to travel across academic audiences rather than sit within a single discipline.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest impact signal is use: the book has recorded 1,957 downloads, indicating sustained reader interest and access in the open-distribution environment. It is available as open access in hybrid form, which likely supports that reach. In addition, the book is linked to the SDG theme Reduced inequalities, suggesting that its framing resonates beyond purely descriptive neighbourhood studies and into broader debates about inclusion and social justice.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication record also shows formal support from several institutions, including KU Leuven, the Institute of Modern Languages Research at the University of London, the Humanities Institute at University College Dublin, and the Irish Research Council. This pattern points to collaborative scholarly backing across institutions and countries, fitting the book\u2019s transnational theme. The presence of peer-review metadata further indicates a conventional scholarly publication pathway.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the citation record remains sparse: OpenAlex currently reports zero citations. That does not diminish the book\u2019s relevance, but it does mean the evidence base is still centered more on use and positioning than on demonstrated scholarly uptake. The available record therefore suggests an accessible, well-supported book with clear thematic relevance, while its longer-term citation impact is still in the early stages of being established.</p>",
